            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "KAIBRYN"
            },
            "reviewBody": "learned this as a child , &amp; taught it to mine . The only difference is that we also use 1 cup milk (batter will be thin but thats fine). You can adapt the recipe to fit your pan merely by increasing in equal amts. , but we do not ever use more than 1 stick of butter or margarine . you can use any fruit you choose , but if using canned , drain some of the liquid if there's a lot . My favorite is peach ....I sometimes sprinkle a bit of cinnamon on top of that one . This cobbler bakes up into a great cake-like dessert that's wonderful served warm with a scoop of ice cream on top !"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "ProudGin4036"
            },
            "reviewBody": "If you increase the milk until you get a thick pancake batter, it works pretty good, and the recipe becomes easy to remember: stick of butter, cup of sugar, cup of SR flour, cup of milk and 2 cups of berries; easy peazy. I use frozen blackberries in sugar, so I drain off the juice and dust the berries with flour. I made it with only 3/4 cup of milk once but it was WAY too thick.",
            "datePublished": "2023-05-11T23:08:56.703Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Dana Miller Lester"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I have an abundance of blackberries this season so I thought I'd give this a try. It was good, my family quite enjoyed it. I did change a few things. I used half white sugar and half light brown sugar and I added a teaspoonful of vanilla to the batter. I also melted my butter in the baking dish while the oven was pre-heating. Very easy to prepare, I will probably use this recipe again.",
            "datePublished": "2024-07-07T17:59:04.653Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "MrsB"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I added a Tsp of vanilla to the batter and it was perfect. I put the stick of butter in my baking in the oven for a minute. When it melts, I swirl it up the sides and then pour the batter in.",
            "datePublished": "2023-07-23T22:18:20.202Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "sonnyboy"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Great recipe. Wow, is this good. I did add 1 cup of milk as others suggested. Turned out great. I didn't have fresh fruit, but I had frozen blackberries and frozen blueberries in the freezer. Thawed them and they worked great. While thawing, I tossed them with a little bit of sugar and went from there. I can't believe how well this turned out, because I was leery if the berries would really sink to the bottom. They did and I ate most of it in one day. I know, for shame on me, but.....",
            "datePublished": "2018-04-17T11:09:59.623Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Karin Lang Walker"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Since none of my pans are a standard size, I had to experiment. A doubled recipe (4 cups of berries) fits 3 8\"x4\" loaf pans. I also cut back the sugar to 2/3 c per 2 c of berries, since my berries are so sweet. I like a little tartness in my berry cobblers! I've also made this with canned prepared cherries, skipping the sugar altogether.\nAwesome!!",
            "datePublished": "2017-08-11T21:18:32.080Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "FlashyChai6999"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Note that it calls for self-rising flour. If you don’t have you can add 1 1/2 tsps of baking powder and 1/2 tsps of salt. Will definitely make again",
            "datePublished": "2023-06-02T23:52:10.713Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "JB"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Delicious! I melted the butter in the pan in the oven as it was preheating; didn’t have self-rising flour so substituted with one cup all-purpose flour, 1.5 teaspoons of baking powder and .5 tsp salt.",
            "datePublished": "2018-07-16T01:23:20.453Z"
          },
          {
